Based on direct inspection of the repo (not just the issue JSON), here's my analysis and decision.

## What I found

**The scaffold's own progress data is the smoking gun.** `dynamic-access-exhaust-report.json` shows 111 classes already completed with zero failures/skips — but only 8 of those 111 (7%) are real `org.apache.pulsar.*` classes. The other 103 are shaded third-party internals bundled inside the fat jar (Jackson, Guava, Avro reflection, etc.) that the generator fell back to because it couldn't exercise the library's actual purpose. None of the genuine Pulsar classes reached so far are the runtime classes that matter (`ProducerImpl`, `ConsumerImpl`, `ClientCnx`, binary-protocol `Commands`, schema/admin clients) — those only activate when a producer/consumer actually completes a handshake with a broker.

**Direct repo precedent confirms the fix.** `com.rabbitmq.client:amqp-client` and `com.rabbitmq:amqp-client` (also message-broker clients) hit the same wall and were given a `required-docker-images.txt` pinning `rabbitmq:4.3` (already in the allow-list as `Dockerfile-rabbitmq`), with test code spinning the broker up directly via `ProcessBuilder("docker","run",...)` in `@BeforeAll`/`@AfterAll` and polling readiness before running assertions. `io.nats:jnats` follows the same pattern. Pulsar is architecturally identical — a client whose value is almost entirely in broker interaction.

**Verified the exact image tag exists**: `apachepulsar/pulsar:4.2.5` is published on Docker Hub (matches the client version exactly, avoiding protocol drift), and Testcontainers' own official `PulsarContainer` module uses `bin/pulsar standalone --no-functions-worker -nss` as its startup command — confirming those flags are the right way to skip functions-worker/BookKeeper stream-storage overhead that the client doesn't need.

`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`
The `dynamic-access-exhaust-report.json`'s completedClasses list is effectively a trace of what the generator could reach without extra setup — filtering out the `.shade.` entries turns it into direct evidence of "stuck on X" rather than guesswork. This is a more reliable signal than reading the library's docs in isolation, because it reflects what *this specific scaffold* already tried.
`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`

## Decision

